David Michael Kass <dk...@cco.caltech.edu> wrote:
)Michael Constant <mco...@soda.CSUA.Berkeley.EDU> wrote:
)>Oh no, a single question from the Monocle can be devastating. Suppose
)>I ask you, "is it true that you will either answer no to this question
)>or never harm me for the rest of the game".
)
)As you point out, the only possible answer to this question is yes. Thus
)it is not a question answerable by yes or no since no is not a possible
)answer. Therefore you cannot ask the question with the Monocle.

Based on the wording on the card, you may be right... but replace my
question with "is the answer that you will give to this question the
same as the correct answer to the question `will you never harm me for
the rest of the game'" and my point stands. (If you don't want to
bother figuring it out, the upshot of this question is that you can
answer either yes or no, but either answer forces you to never harm
me for the rest of the game.)

)>In case you haven't figured it out already, this is insanely powerful.
)>I would therefore recommend that everyone play with the recommendation
)>issued by WotC: that the questions must pertain directly to the game.
)>This means that any questions which refer to themselves, other questions,
)>or someone's personal life (I really should try that one sometime :-))
)>are treated as illegal.
)>
)>The Monocle is a cool card when you don't abuse it; let's not force WotC
)>to ban it or take it out of print.
)
)I agree with your points. The problem is that too many people do not have
)the formal logic background to keep it from having its power. I suspect
)that the best solution is to issue errata that the question can only pertain
)to the current turn (or maybe any player's next turn).

You missed the point of my post... I will restate it.

---> I would therefore recommend that everyone play with the recommendation
---> issued by WotC: that the questions must pertain directly to the game.

I think that this is the best solution.
--
Michael Constant (mco...@soda.csua.berkeley.edu)
